  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x+{x}^{2/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=2+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4x+1, & x<0 \\\\ -4x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{\u2032}(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4, & x<0 \\\\ -4, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\sqrt[5]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{6/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{6}{5}{x}^{1/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{\u2032}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"",
